    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "### Problems\n\n1. `'guicursor'` is not respected when extra cursors exist, even for the main cursor. \n2. Upon exiting visual mode after moving extra cursors in visual mode, the extra cursors snap back to where they were before, unlike the main cursor.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n#### (1) 'guicursor' \n- `nvim --clean`\n- Create the following `init.lua`:\n  ```lua\n  vim.opt.guicursor:append('v:VisualCursor')\n  vim.api.nvim_set_hl(0, 'VisualCursor', { bg = 0xFF0000 })\n  ```\n- Create extra cursors with `Q`, then enter visual mode with `v`. The main cursor will not be red.\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/6e0d03ca-ddc2-4899-a062-7ee7e92f295c\n\n#### (2) extra cursors snap back\n- `nvim --clean`\n- Create extra cursor with `Q`, then enter visual mode with `v`, move around with `h`/`l`, then hit `<Esc>`. The extra cursors will incorrectly snap back.\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/56860629-9c04-4369-b858-97f4dc08f8e7\n\n### Expected behaviors\n\n1. `'guicursor'` works with multicursor\n2. Extra cursors behave the same as the main cursor when exiting visual mode\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nNVIM v0.13.0-dev-1489+gf1d89e874a",
